+ {"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-25", "title": "[P3] A same-id downgrade to warn loses the rule's block_message - the user is told `would have blocked: Command blocked by security policy.` instead of why", "priority": "P3", "cell": "interruptor warn mode", "detail": "With a same-id override (`action: warn`) in rules.d, the verdict the CLI prints is `terminal-jail: WARNING - would have blocked: Command blocked by security policy.` The generic placeholder replaces the rule's own block_message, so the warning does not say which policy was downgraded or what it protects - and warn mode exists precisely so an operator can decide whether the downgrade is safe. The engine does emit the right rule_id. Fix direction: carry the matched rule's block_message through the warn path instead of the generic string, and add a bridge test asserting the downgraded rule's message appears in `reason`.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, same-id override verification", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
